/ref 17592186365213 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124645 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186435022 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124645 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186483396 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124645 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Extreme Values of a Constrained Quadratic Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124645 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186124208 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124645 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "639785dd-cc89-52aa-84a2-84fc139c979d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124738 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125553 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124738 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125555 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124738 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125560 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124738 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125624 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124738 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125627 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124738 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124738 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124738 #edb/ref 1045 "Lagrange Multipliers With One Constraint" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124738 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186125553 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124738 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124738 #edb/ref 1050 1200.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124738 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "67f5c7b4-d6b9-56a1-a476-c05d63882f58" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124816 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186121493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124816 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162761" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124816 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124816 #edb/ref 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,$ on the hyperbola ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-1=0.$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the global minimum of $f(x,y)$ exists on the hyperbola.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124816 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "794a3e0d-09de-59e6-90ac-90bc491d764c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124816 #edb/ref 1074 "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-1=0.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2(x-1),2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,-2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2(x-1) &=2\\lambda x \\\\ 2y &=-2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x(1-\\lambda ) &=1 \\\\ y(\\lambda +1) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-1=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-1 & =0\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(-1,0),(1,0).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =-1$ in the second equation of the system, the first equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(1+1)=1\\,⇒\\,x=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-1=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}=-\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives a contradiction.\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-1,0),\\,(1,0).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(-1,0)$ | $(1,0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $4$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at the point $(1,0)$ and its minimum value is $0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186124817 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281141 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124817 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162762" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124817 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124817 #edb/ref 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}\\,$ on the ellipse ${x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2=0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124817 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "83f506ab-232e-59b7-85e6-ab9697a12cb0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124817 #edb/ref 1074 "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2=0.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,2y+4⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,4y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x &=2\\lambda x \\\\ 2y+4 &=4\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x(1-\\lambda ) &=0 \\\\ y(2\\lambda -1) &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2}-2 & =0\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,-1),(0,1).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =1$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(2(1)-1)=2\\,⇒\\,y=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives a contradiction.\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,-1),\\,(0,1).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,-1)$ | $(0,1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $1$ | $9$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at the point $(0,1)$ and its maximum value is $9.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186125264 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125264 #edb/ref 1069 "q-99113" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125264 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125264 #edb/ref 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=x+3y-z$ on the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=11.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125264 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "67c90c79-df35-57ea-bcc9-2e5840258d44" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125264 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to find the minimum value of $f$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=11.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=11\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,3,-1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1 &=2\\lambda x \\\\ 3 &=2\\lambda y \\\\ -1 &=2\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{1}{2\\lambda } \\\\ y &=\\frac{3}{2\\lambda } \\\\ z &=-\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x,\\,y$ and $z$ into the condition $g(x,y,z)=11$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =11 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{9}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =11 \\\\ \\frac{11}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =11 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x,\\,y$ and $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm 1,\\,y=\\pm 3,\\,z=∓1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points \n\n$$\n(1,3,-1),\\,(-1,-3,1).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y,z)$ | $(1,3,-1)$ | $(-1,-3,1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y,z)$ | $11$ | $-11$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-1,-3,1),$ and its minimum value is $-11.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186125553 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125627 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125553 #edb/ref 1061 32055 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125553 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "639b5b29-18f2-5454-b193-c76dd6a45f3a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125553 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125555 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125624 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125555 #edb/ref 1061 32291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125555 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ba4c5cbe-9bb4-5323-8a0f-c6fcc06d7cc5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125555 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124609 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125560 #edb/ref 1061 32494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125560 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "242a9cf3-c2e5-570a-b9f0-936162411df1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125560 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124612 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125624 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125560 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125624 #edb/ref 1061 44830 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125624 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6e09518a-865f-5121-9684-78bd4bfcab56" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125624 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124645 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125627 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125627 #edb/ref 1061 44868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125627 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0d14ea94-0983-5096-b7d2-ed0dc8de4dce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125627 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125872 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125660 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125555 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125660 #edb/ref 1061 45437 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125660 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "66d693a8-cc66-5185-8b5f-4ba65d4664e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125660 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125894 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125845 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we would like to find the minimum and maximum values of the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = 2x^2+4y^2\n$$\n\nsubject to constraint $g(x,y) = x^2+y^2=1.$ In other words, we wish to find the global extrema of $f(x,y)$ defined on the circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, as shown below.\n\n![](images/39/39893af9755488d142116370d451b7a3d92b334c27f983eb302492b0d516c41c.png)\n\nFirst, let's draw our constraint circle ($x^2+y^2=1$) and consider the level curves of $f(x,y).$ These level curves are ellipses of the form\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = 2x^2+4y^2 = k.\n$$\n\nAs we increase the parameter $k,$ we get ellipses with larger and larger axes, as shown below. \n\n![](images/d5/d59d566c9cf7e252884a1397eb4ac0c8f85b290790e9063be2ff6eb4a4aff04a.png)\n\nAs we continue to increase $k,$ a level curve will touch the circle at the points $(\\pm 1, 0)$ for some value of $k.$ The value of $k$ corresponding to that level curve gives us the *smallest* possible value of $f(x,y)$ on our circle $x^2+y^2=1.$\n\nIncreasing $k$ even further, we'll get another value of $k$ for which the corresponding level curve touches the circle at the points $(0, \\pm 1),$ as shown below. This value of $k$ gives us the *largest* possible value of the function $f(x,y)$ on our circle.\n\n![](images/48/48f71f5aa237936be24ccba572fb650b0d477c252d8338549a33cfea4874cbad.png)\n\nNotice that the corresponding level curves touch the circle in both cases (the minimum and the maximum). So, the level curve $f(x,y)=k$ and the circle $g(x,y)=1$ have a common tangent at these points. This means that the *gradients* of both curves must be parallel at these points. Therefore, at the extreme points, we must have\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda$ is some real number. The number $\\lambda$ is called a **Lagrange multiplier**.\n\nWe can now use these observations to write down a general method for optimizing a function subject to a constraint." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125845 #edb/ref 1065 5109 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125845 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "932667c3-77f7-56cb-ae08-dd6835e29462" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125845 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125872 #edb/ref 1064 "The **method of Lagrange multipliers** states that the extrema of a function $f(x,y)$ subject to constraint $g(x,y)=c$ can be found by solving the system\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y), \\qquad g(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nThe method of Lagrange multipliers assumes that $\\nabla g \\neq \\mathbf{0}$ on the curve $g(x,y)=c.$\n\nNote that solutions to this system do not always exist! For example, the function $f(x,y)=x+y$ does not have a minimum (or maximum) on the hyperbola $x^2-y^2=1.$\n\nHowever, we can use the following theorem to guarantee the existence of solutions in some cases:\n\n*If $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint function $g(x,y) = c$ is closed and bounded, then $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125872 #edb/ref 1065 7750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125872 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "906426bb-122c-5b43-a304-d63921ab20dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125872 #edb/ref 1067 "The Method of Lagrange Multipliers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125894 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's now consider our previous example, where we wish to find the extrema of the function $f(x,y)=2x^2+4y^2$ on the circle $g(x,y)=x^2+y^2=1.$\n\nThe method of Lagrange multipliers states that we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y), \\qquad g(x,y)=c\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨4x,8y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x &=2\\lambda x \\\\ 8y &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x(2-\\lambda ) &=0 \\\\ y(4-\\lambda ) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation, the constraint condition $x^2+y^2=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(0,\\pm 1 \\right).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =2$ in the first equation, the second equation implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(4-2)=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition $x^2+y^2=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{0}^{2} & =1\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(\\pm 1, 0 \\right).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(\\pm 1, 0), \\qquad (0, \\pm1).\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $f$ at these points:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(\\pm 1,0)$ | $(0, \\pm 1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum of $2$ at the points $(\\pm 1, 0)$ and a maximum of $4$ at points $(0, \\pm 1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186125894 #edb/ref 1065 7881 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125894 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a5ffe247-960a-5ed1-92ae-ae8c917cbefc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125894 #edb/ref 1067 "A Worked Example" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278746 #edb/ref 1080 "-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278746 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278746 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "10e57bce-4288-5fe3-aa0a-73628c52743d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278823 #edb/ref 1080 "-14" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278823 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278823 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "19f53dcb-0e32-5ceb-8aaf-a210c8dc8521" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278848 #edb/ref 1080 "-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278848 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278848 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1b9bf5ef-0b2f-58e3-a02b-38b0b94d9f87" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186279232 #edb/ref 1080 "16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186279232 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186279232 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "479580a8-ae4a-5bac-b57e-907136b973ca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186279446 #edb/ref 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186279446 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186279446 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5d3b843e-b72a-51a2-a995-da57e46e487b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186279539 #edb/ref 1080 "5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186279539 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186279539 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "68331d3b-2043-56d0-817d-9396a163baef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186279754 #edb/ref 1080 "-3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186279754 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186279754 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "81d5aaa0-fd9f-568f-9bb1-e082fcdaa7de" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186280097 #edb/ref 1080 "-6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186280097 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186280097 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a999c752-b365-5d3b-960d-66d2a2fbec26" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186280130 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 1759218